 ND OH OK PA RI SD TX WV WI) that all said the same thing; CT IL ME MA NJ NY from sp-<st>-who-1."}},
{"lib": "field", "chunk": {"id": "fed-811-wait-1", "state": "US", "tier": "core", "title": "811 wait before digging, by state: does my state use 2 working days, 48 hours, 72 hours or 3 days?", "topic": "811 dig rules by state: wait before digging, ticket life, tolerance zone", "text": "There is no federal 811 wait time, ticket life or tolerance zone: each state's one-call law sets its own numbers. The federal floor everywhere is 49 CFR 196 for digging near pipelines: use one-call first, wait for the marks, dig with care, report damage, call 911 for a release. Wait after the call, from each state's card (use only the job state's line; the state card has the exact counting rule). 2 working or business days: Alabama, Alaska, Arizona, Arkansas, California, Colorado, Connecticut, Florida, Georgia, Idaho, Illinois, Indiana, Kansas, Kentucky, Missouri, Nebraska (app table, not re-read), Nevada, New Mexico, New York, Ohio, Oregon, Washington, Wyoming. 48 hours, weekends and holidays not counted: Iowa, Louisiana, Minnesota, North Dakota, Oklahoma, South Dakota, Texas, Utah, Virginia, West Virginia. 72 hours: Maine, Massachusetts, Michigan, New Hampshire, Rhode Island, Vermont. 3 working or business days: Delaware, Maryland, New Jersey, North Carolina, Pennsylvania, South Carolina, Tennessee, Wisconsin. 96 hours: District of Columbia. 5 working days: Hawaii. Not confirmed: Mississippi, Montana.", "source": "Each state's dig-<state> card in this library (state one-call laws and 811 center handbooks, read 2026-11-20); 49 CFR 196.103-196.109", "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-196", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "State laws as read 2026-11-20; 49 CFR 196 current to Oct 22, 2026", "confidence": "medium", "kind": "summary", "license": "gov-us", "unconfirmed": "yes"}},
{"lib": "field", "chunk": {"id": "fed-811-ticketlife-1", "state": "US", "tier": "core", "title": "811 ticket life by state: how long is a locate ticket good for in my state?", "topic": "811 dig rules by state: wait before digging, ticket life, tolerance zone", "text": "There is no federal 811 wait time, ticket life or tolerance zone: each state's one-call law sets its own numbers. The federal floor everywhere is 49 CFR 196 for digging near pipelines: use one-call first, wait for the marks, dig with care, report damage, call 911 for a release. Ticket life, from each state's card (use only the job state's line; days are calendar, working or business days as its card says). 14 days: Minnesota, Oklahoma, Texas (working days). 15: Arizona, District of Columbia, New Mexico, South Carolina, Tennessee, Virginia. 20: Alabama, Arkansas, Indiana, Kansas, Louisiana, Mississippi. 21: Kentucky, Michigan, North Dakota, South Dakota, Utah. 25: Iowa, Illinois. 28: California, Hawaii, Nevada, North Carolina. 30: Colorado, Florida, Georgia, Massachusetts, New Hampshire, Oregon, Vermont, Connecticut (app table, not confirmed). 4 weeks: Idaho. 45: New Jersey (business days), Washington. 60: Maine. 12 business days: Delaware, Maryland. Each 14 business days: Wyoming. No set expiry: Alaska, New York, Rhode Island (while marks are kept up), Wisconsin, Ohio (none given, not confirmed). Not confirmed: Missouri, Montana, Nebraska, Pennsylvania, West Virginia.", "source": "Each state's dig-<state> card in this library (state one-call laws and 811 center handbooks, read 2026-11-20); 49 CFR 196.103-196.109", "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-196", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "State laws as read 2026-11-20; 49 CFR 196 current to Oct 22, 2026", "confidence": "medium", "kind": "summary", "license": "gov-us", "unconfirmed": "yes"}},
{"lib": "field", "chunk": {"id": "fed-811-zone-1", "state": "US", "tier": "core", "title": "811 tolerance zone (hand-dig zone) by state: 18 inches or 24 inches in my state?", "topic": "811 dig rules by state: wait before digging, ticket life, tolerance zone", "text": "There is no federal 811 wait time, ticket life or tolerance zone: each state's one-call law sets its own numbers. The federal floor everywhere is 49 CFR 196 for digging near pipelines: use one-call first, wait for the marks, dig with care, report damage, call 911 for a release. Tolerance zone, from each state's card (use only the job state's line; measured from the mark, the edge or plus the line's width as its card says). 18 inches: Alabama (from 2027), Colorado, District of Columbia, Georgia, Illinois, Iowa, Louisiana, Maine, Maryland (not re-read), Massachusetts, Mississippi, Nebraska, New Hampshire, New Mexico, Ohio, Pennsylvania, Rhode Island, South Dakota, Vermont, Wisconsin, Texas (pipelines, plus half the diameter). 24 inches or 2 feet: Alaska, Arizona, California, Delaware, Florida, Idaho, Minnesota, Nevada, New York, North Carolina, North Dakota, Oklahoma, Oregon, South Carolina, Utah, Washington, Wyoming; Kansas, New Jersey, Tennessee, Virginia (in the statute, not re-read). 48 inch caution zone: Michigan. Not confirmed: Arkansas, Connecticut, Hawaii, Indiana, Kentucky, Missouri, Montana, West Virginia.", "source": "Each state's dig-<state> card in this library (state one-call laws and 811 center handbooks, read 2026-11-20); 49 CFR 196.103-196.109", "url": "https://www.ecfr.gov/current/title-49/subtitle-B/chapter-I/subchapter-D/part-196", "checked": "2026-11-20", "edition": "State laws as read 2026-11-20; 49 CFR 196 current to Oct 22, 2026", "confidence": "medium", "kind": "summary", "license": "gov-us", "unconfirmed": "yes"}}]$kb$::jsonb) with ordinality t(e, i);
